# In a Town Without a Sugar Mill...

## Anna on Agitations

### The Anti-Hindi Agitation

### False Reports About the DMK Given to Periyar

Younger brother!

You are terribly mischievous! While a great "agitation" was taking shape, you remained silent. Very well, let us suppose that bewilderment made you do so. But I thought that, at least now, when the agitation is over and its "results" are being reckoned, you would offer your comments. You, however, refuse even to take notice that such an agitation occurred. You are very mischievous indeed!

Do you know? Because we did not take part in that agitation, they abuse us as "cowards"!

What is it, younger brother? You are laughing. Perhaps you are saying, "Is this anything new? We have heard insults far more degrading and venomous than the word coward. What does this amount to?"

That is true. The comrades of our party have matured!

The stage at which a slight word provoked rage, and criticism produced an outburst, has passed.

We have to answer only to our own conscience, not to those disposed to call us traitors. To learn whether there is fever in the body, we must examine our own body; we do not do it by touching either the kitchen stove or a block of ice.

Cowards! We are cowards!

The policemen carrying batons will laugh!

The prison officials will laugh!

If the scars of sacrifice we have received could do so, they would laugh thunderously and mock these people!

Younger brother, in 1942 the Congress conducted the August agitation by such methods as removing railway tracks and burning post offices. We did not participate. By "we," younger brother, I do not mean only you and me. The Dravidar Kazhagam and the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam were then one; I mean that entire family. Did that signify that we were cowards who fled in fear of repression? We sincerely believed those methods were wrong. We thought that futile strife, disorder, confusion, and destruction of property would be all that remained. We therefore stood aside. In the same way, the DMK has not now taken part in burning the flag. How can it be appropriate to call this cowardice?

If one wishes, one may merely conclude that this is customary in Tamil Nadu politics: without enough affection or goodwill even to learn another's view, people devise a programme and then abuse as cowards those who decline to join it. Congressmen abused Dravidians as cowards for not joining their August agitation. In the current agitations too, whether it is flag-burning or any other struggle, we are abused as cowards if we do not join. Can this be regarded as meaningful? It must be taken simply as a long-standing custom and habit.

We have an organisation of our own. We have gathered, discussed matters, and conducted several agitations. Efforts to conduct more continue. Have you ever wondered why, ignoring all this, they say that we must join every agitation begun by others and condemn us when we do not? It means that every camp believes we possess the maturity, training, and ability to work exceedingly well. That is why every eye turns towards us!

Yes, younger brother. The three-front struggle demonstrated clearly that we can withstand even the harshest agitation. That is why, whoever begins whatever struggle, they eagerly expect us to come. Their eagerness grows; when it is not fulfilled, their anger rises, and they hurl condemnations. What else can it be?

It is good to conduct oneself in a way that pleases others; that is refinement too. But there is also such a thing as self-respect, including the self-respect of an organisation. Only when it has been invited and has participated in framing the programme can such a position endure and flourish.

The battle is over! Its results are known! What do we see, younger brother? Kamaraj's statement!

Mavalankar, Speaker of the Union Parliament, said at Salem on 31 May that Hindi would not be introduced through compulsion. (Mail, 1 June.)

"We are not imposing Hindi and will not impose it. We encourage people to study Hindi but do not compel them," Union Labour Minister Khandubhai Desai stated at Thrissur on 28 May. (Mail, 29 May.)

Union Deputy Home Minister Datar stated that Hindi would be spread carefully so that those seeking employment would not face difficulty. This can be seen in the Mail of 4 May.

At Puri on 11 May, Union Minister Keskar declared that there was no question of forcibly imposing Hindi upon the people. This appeared in The Hindu.

"We will not impose Hindi or make it compulsory. I shall wait until the people of Tamil Nadu acquire proficiency in Hindi of their own accord," Union Minister Keskar said at Madurai on 6 July. The statement appeared in the Mail.

It was only in July, at Hyderabad, that President Babu Rajendra Prasad said it was not the Government's intention to impose Hindi upon South India.

Pandit Nehru has on many occasions condemned the conduct of Hindi zealots.

The Pandit has repeatedly said that no language can equal one's mother tongue.

Even in North India, many leaders from Bengal, Maharashtra, Kalinga, and Punjab have spoken against Hindi fanaticism.

Whenever functions connected with language or education are held, the rulers of the Delhi Durbar themselves say that Hindi will not be imposed. They will continue to say so.

As far as Tamil Nadu is concerned, we may state categorically that compulsory Hindi died after taking the lives of Thalamuthu and Natarajan.

The Central Government has clarified its position on this matter many times. The state governments have been given the right to act "as they deem fit."

That is why the Andhra state government was able to make Hindi a compulsory subject. Pointing to Andhra, the Central Government did not ask why the Madras state had not made Hindi compulsory.

"Compulsory Hindi" is not today a programme of the Central Government, nor has the Madras state adopted it as a programme. No such intention or effort exists.

In these circumstances, why do we need a promise that Hindi will neither be made a compulsory subject nor imposed upon us?

We therefore cannot bring ourselves to rejoice that, by announcing the burning of the flag and creating an atmosphere of war, we obtained the "boon" called Kamaraj's statement. Nor do we wish to diminish Periyar's greatness by claiming that this deed added to it. His present friends and collaborators are performing that task efficiently, and are even being paid for their trouble!

Those friends give Periyar profoundly false accounts of the DMK. They conceal the truth.

According to their calculations, the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam consists of half a dozen boys!

It lies somewhere in Royapuram!

For six years they have continued to offer the "auspicious prophecy" that it will dissolve and disperse, crumble and decline, grow frail, and die!

They delight Periyar by spreading wild tales that this man has left it and that man is about to leave.

Periyar asks: "You say those fellows have no influence at all. Yet they hold conferences and show a surplus of thousands or tens of thousands. How is that possible?" Does he stop there? He asks, "You too fill me with tremendous confidence and hold conferences. But when I ask about the surplus, you say there is still a little to be paid to the pavilion contractor and the band!" What can they do? What can they say to escape?

"That is... they raise money by displaying cinema and drama people... that is why..." they drawl. He looks at them angrily and clears his throat. There is meaning in that. Do not the processions at their conferences proceed with Nadigavel M. R. Radha seated upon a horse? Would he not know it? If the DMK shines because cinema people participate in it, should support not swell and overflow towards the place where Nadigavel, admired even by cinema artists themselves, stands?

In this manner, every truth is concealed from him.

"They will disappear by January! When March comes they will be dead. In June a fatal fever will seize them; in July they will be gone; August will destroy them altogether!" Their performance resembles that of a sorcerer who smears turmeric and vermilion on a hen's egg, mutters something over it, and says, "I shall bury this in a three-foot pit in the cattle shed. Within three hours you will see him die with blood pouring from his mouth and nose." He collects his fee, ties it into his cloth, and goes away to drink himself senseless. In just this fashion, they tell Periyar, "Look, I shall finish them off with seven articles and three speeches," and ask him to believe them.

In every town they name prominent people and lie to Periyar: "He belongs to our party and is deeply devoted to you. He will not come out openly, but inwardly he is firmly attached to the principles. There may be a vermilion mark on his forehead, but he cannot sleep without reading Kudi Arasu."

Periyar asks, "How many people do those fellows have in that town?" At once they reply that the DMK does not have even one person there.

"We shall picket North Indian shops. They will not participate. Then, leaving the North Indian shopkeeper aside, we shall pounce upon them and finish them off. Nine articles will be enough," they say. They write ninety-nine articles, but the DMK shows no sign of vanishing. Afraid that if they rest they will waste away, they write a few more articles and console themselves: "Even if these do not help destroy them, at least let our habit of writing improve." Then they go to Periyar, grin, and say, "For one who has written so much, give me another twenty!"

It is not that Periyar is unaware of these things!

But what can be done? As the saying goes, in a town without a sugar mill, the blossom of the iluppai tree passes for sugar! Do you understand, younger brother?

Affectionately,

14 August 1955
